Z||000007||BN Boomer||06-19-2000||05:52 AM||||My understanding of flywheel hp vs. net hp is that net hp ratings simply account for the power typically lost during transmission through the drivetrain to the driven wheels. The weight of the vehicle has no factor in determining the rating, the efficiency of the drivetrain does.||||reg||1
Z||000008||phillip||06-19-2000||09:38 AM||||i'm thinking net hp won't change, but if the fiero is lighter you would have a better power to weight ratio.<P>stimpy, if thats all it takes some corvette owner's gonna be missing his zr-1 badge. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||06-19-2000||09:53 AM||||The 3400 engine should be a very good choice for the Fiero. The aluminum heads will save some weight and breathe much better than the iron heads. The roller cam gives a much wider  torque curve too. The Fiero's shorter exhaust should help boost horsepower too. BTW, the Grand Am GT has the same engine, but with 180 hp, the only difference being a more open intake. Also, there have been several concept vehicles with this engine that ran almost 240hp. Just to top it all off, it shouldn't be too hard to add on the Rotrez supercharger to boost output up to around 300 hp. Sounds like a nice upgrade path, a few new goodies every year?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
