Z||000014||ThatRickGuy||06-19-2000||11:19 PM||||speaking of ricers... would it be concidered a "rice boy" action to do a notch back to fast back conversion on a formula and keep the formula decals?<P>as far as net vs gross hp and the car weight. Physics can't lie (altho, in extreme cases can be bent) and the hp will still the same in either car, BN Boomer nailed it, gross is at the fly wheel, but you'll never see that at the wheel. There is power loss from there back, the torque converter, tranny, and differential all cause a bit of it, I've seen pretty good ways of calculating it, dealing w/ some much hp per individual piece up to certain percents.. but it's confussing to think about. but... putting that same 180 net hp in a car 1/2 a ton lighter will make you go faster. I heard a rough approximations of 100 lbs = .1 sec in the 1/4 mile, so, exact same engines you should be able to take a car, 1000 lbs (1/2 ton) by 1 second in the 1/4. That why I find it more impressive to see a 1970 gto run a 13 second 1/4 then a civic &lt;gag&gt; run a 12.||||reg||1
